{"qid":"Q804","names":["Republic of Panama"],"title":"Republic of Panama","endonym":{"native":"República de Panamá","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Panama declared independence from Colombia on 3 November 1903, backed by US support motivated by canal-route interests; the Hay-Bunau-Varilla Treaty, signed just days later, granted the United States perpetual control over the Canal Zone. The completed Panama Canal opened on 15 August 1914 and transformed the republic into a global transit hub. The country endured military rule under Omar Torrijos (1968–1981) and Manuel Noriega (effectively 1983–1989), the latter removed by the US invasion of December 1989. Full sovereignty over the Canal Zone was transferred to Panama on 31 December 1999 under the Torrijos-Carter Treaties of 1977.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Panama's internationally recognised borders are well established; the maritime boundary with Colombia in the Caribbean was clarified by treaty in 1976, and the Darien frontier is defined though ecologically remote.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Panama","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panama"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Panama Canal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panama_Canal"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Torrijos–Carter Treaties","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torrijos%E2%80%93Carter_Treaties"}],"britannica":"place/Panama","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Panama","rulers":[{"qid":"Q322234","name":"Federico Boyd","start":1910,"end":1910,"image":"FBoyd4.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/FBoyd4.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist